> Derive the prefix invariant and stability

Before inserting position i, positions [0,i) are the stable sorted order of the
first i original records; later positions are untouched. Holding a[i] opens one
slot. Every predecessor strictly larger than the key moves right, preserving its
order relative to the other shifted records. The first predecessor no larger
than the key ends the scan, and key placement produces the stable sorted prefix
[0,i+1). Equality stops shifting, so a later equal record never crosses an
earlier one. Induction proves a stable sorted permutation after the last
insertion.

Original identities travel with the actual assignments in the observed call.
They are observations of the integer algorithm, not a separate generic payload
sorting API. The result's original indices make otherwise invisible equal-key
order checkable.

> Exact work is controlled by inversions

A strict inversion is a pair j<i with a[j]>a[i]. On insertion i, exactly those
larger earlier values shift right. Thus the total number of shifts is exactly
the original inversion count I. Each insertion also writes the held key once,
even when its destination is unchanged. For n>0 there are n−1 placements.

Let M count strict new minima after the first input: an a[i] smaller than every
preceding value. Such an insertion reaches the left boundary and has no final
false key test; every other insertion has one. Therefore, for n>0,

$$
S=I,\qquad P=n-1,\qquad C=I+(n-1)-M.
$$
\alttext(Actual shifts equal inversions, placements equal n minus one, and comparisons add one failed key test except for strict new minima.)

Empty input has S=P=C=0. Already sorted input, including all equal values, has
I=M=0 and C=n−1. A strictly reversed distinct input has I=n(n−1)/2 and M=n−1, so
C=I. Index-bound checks, held-key reads, input copies, identity bookkeeping,
allocation and observations are excluded from these selected counts. Logical
value-array writes are S+P, not a byte-traffic or physical-storage endurance
model.

The body runs in Θ(n+I), hence Θ(n) when I=O(n), with a quadratic worst case.
The legacy by-value API additionally owns/copies its input/output, while its
body uses constant auxiliary value storage. Observed identities add O(n); full
snapshots add Θ(n²) values and corresponding observation time/storage.

